1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,808
Thanks for coming everyone.

2
00:00:02,808 --> 00:00:08,180
OK, so this meeting is for new staff and staff

3
00:00:08,180 --> 00:00:11,700
who haven't been involved with our volunteering projects yet.

4
00:00:11,700 --> 00:00:22,400
So basically, the idea is that we allow staff to give up some of their work time to help on various charity projects to benefit the local community.

5
00:00:22,400 --> 00:00:27,300
We've been doing this for the last five years and it's been very successful.

6
00:00:27,300 --> 00:00:31,211
Participating doesn't necessarily involve a huge time commitment.

7
00:00:31,211 --> 00:00:36,100
The company will pay for eight hours of your time.

8
00:00:36,100 --> 00:00:40,280
That can be used over one or two days all at once

9
00:00:40,280 --> 00:00:44,400
or spread over several months throughout the year.

10
00:00:44,400 --> 00:00:46,879
There are some staff who enjoy volunteering

11
00:00:46,879 --> 00:00:52,900
so much they also give up their own free time for a couple of hours every week.

12
00:00:52,900 --> 00:00:55,281
It's completely up to you.

13
00:00:55,281 --> 00:00:58,900
Obviously, many people will have family commitments

14
00:00:58,900 --> 00:01:02,900
and aren't as available as other members of staff.

15
00:01:02,900 --> 00:01:06,900
Feedback from staff has been overwhelmingly positive.

16
00:01:06,900 --> 00:01:10,843
Because they felt they were doing something really useful,

17
00:01:10,843 --> 00:01:16,100
nearly everyone agreed that volunteering made them feel more motivated at work.

18
00:01:16,100 --> 00:01:20,240
They also liked building relationships with the people in the local community

19
00:01:20,240 --> 00:01:22,230
and felt valued by them.

20
00:01:22,230 --> 00:01:28,000
One or two people also said it was a good thing to have on their CVs.

21
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:34,400
One particularly successful project last year was the Get Working Project.

22
00:01:34,400 --> 00:01:39,400
This was aimed at helping unemployed people in the area get back to work.

23
00:01:39,400 --> 00:01:43,400
Our staff were able to help them improve their telephone skills

24
00:01:43,780 --> 00:01:46,517
such as writing down messages

25
00:01:46,517 --> 00:01:48,880
and speaking with confidence to potential customers

26
00:01:49,020 --> 00:01:52,300
which they had found quite difficult.

27
00:01:52,300 --> 00:01:56,387
This is something many employers look for in job applicants -

28
00:01:56,387 --> 00:02:01,700
and something we all do without even thinking about, every day at work.

29
00:02:01,700 --> 00:02:04,987
We've got an exciting new project starting this year.

30
00:02:04,987 --> 00:02:10,100
Up until now, we've mainly focused on projects to do with education and training.

31
00:02:10,100 --> 00:02:13,260
And we'll continue with our reading project in schools

32
00:02:13,260 --> 00:02:16,200
and our work with local charities.

33
00:02:16,200 --> 00:02:22,800
But we've also agreed to help out on a conservation project in Redfern Park.

34
00:02:22,800 --> 00:02:27,080
So if any of you fancy being outside and getting your hands dirty

35
00:02:27,640 --> 00:02:29,800
this is the project for you.

36
00:02:29,800 --> 00:02:33,660
I also want to mention the annual Digital Inclusion Day

37
00:02:33,880 --> 00:02:35,814
which is coming up next month.

38
00:02:35,814 --> 00:02:40,700
The aim of this is to help older people keep up with technology.

39
00:02:40,700 --> 00:02:45,714
And this year, instead of hosting the event in our own training facility,

40
00:02:45,714 --> 00:02:51,500
we're using the ICT suite at Hill College, as it can hold far more people.

41
00:02:51,500 --> 00:02:56,454
We've invited over 60 people from the Silver Age Community Centre to take part,

42
00:02:56,454 --> 00:03:00,700
so we'll need a lot of volunteers to help with this event.

43
00:03:00,700 --> 00:03:03,040
If you're interested in taking part,

44
00:03:03,040 --> 00:03:08,500
please go to the volunteering section of our website and complete the relevant form.

45
00:03:08,500 --> 00:03:11,555
We won't be providing any training for this

46
00:03:11,555 --> 00:03:16,900
but you'll be paired with an experienced volunteer if you've never done it before.

47
00:03:16,900 --> 00:03:24,500
By the way, don't forget to tell your manager about any volunteering activities you decide to do.

48
00:03:24,500 --> 00:03:28,033
The participants on the Digital Inclusion Day really benefited.

49
00:03:28,033 --> 00:03:30,740
The majority were in their seventies

50
00:03:30,860 --> 00:03:35,100
though some were younger and a few were even in their nineties!

51
00:03:35,100 --> 00:03:38,820
Quite a few owned both a computer and a mobile phone

52
00:03:39,100 --> 00:03:42,300
but these tended to be outdated models.

53
00:03:42,300 --> 00:03:44,852
They generally knew how to do simple things,

54
00:03:44,852 --> 00:03:49,000
like send texts, but weren't aware of recent developments in mobile phone technology.

55
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:55,350
A few were keen to learn but most were quite dismissive at first -

56
00:03:55,350 --> 00:04:01,700
they couldn't see the point of updating their skills. But that soon changed.

57
00:04:01,700 --> 00:04:04,600
The feedback was very positive.

58
00:04:04,600 --> 00:04:13,583
The really encouraging thing was that participants all said they felt much more confident about using social media to keep in touch

59
00:04:13,583 --> 00:04:19,300
with their grandchildren, who prefer this form of communication to phoning or sending emails.

60
00:04:19,300 --> 00:04:24,180
A lot of them also said playing online games would help them make new friends

61
00:04:24,180 --> 00:04:26,400
and keep their brains active.

62
00:04:26,400 --> 00:04:30,537
They weren't that impressed with being able to order their groceries online,

63
00:04:30,537 --> 00:04:33,295
as they liked going out to the shops,

64
00:04:33,295 --> 00:04:39,500
but some said it would come in handy if they were ill or the weather was really bad.

65
00:04:39,500 --> 00:04:44,736
One thing they asked about was using tablets for things like reading newspapers -

66
00:04:44,736 --> 00:04:49,972
some people had been given tablets as presents but had never used them,

67
00:04:49,972 --> 00:04:54,000
so that's something we'll make sure we include this time...
